    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\nset protocols isis level 1 disable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done by adding the interface under the protocols isis hierarchy.\\n2. The interface names are slightly different:\\n   - gigabitethernet1/0/0 becomes ge-1/0/0.0 (Juniper adds .0 for unit/interface numbering)\\n   - LoopBack0 becomes lo0.0\\n3. The \"is-level level-1\" from Huawei is translated to \"level 1 disable\" in Juniper, which means disabling level-2 (making it level-1 only)\\n4. The IS-IS instance number (1 in Huawei) isn\\'t explicitly needed in Juniper\\'s basic configuration.\\n\\nNote: The network-entity and other IS-IS parameters would need additional configuration in Juniper to fully match the Huawei setup.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\nset protocols isis level 1 disable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done by adding the interface under the protocols isis hierarchy.\\n2. The interface names are slightly different:\\n   - gigabitethernet1/0/0 becomes ge-1/0/0.0 (Juniper adds .0 for unit/interface numbering)\\n   - LoopBack0 becomes lo0.0\\n3. The \"is-level level-1\" from Huawei is translated to \"level 1 disable\" in Juniper, which means disabling level-2 (making it level-1 only)\\n4. The IS-IS instance number (1 in Huawei) isn\\'t explicitly needed in Juniper\\'s basic configuration.\\n\\nNote: The network-entity and other IS-IS parameters would need additional configuration in Juniper to fully match the Huawei setup.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis level 1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\nset protocols isis level 1 disable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done by adding the interface under the protocols isis hierarchy.\\n2. The interface names are slightly different:\\n   - gigabitethernet1/0/0 becomes ge-1/0/0.0 (Juniper adds .0 for unit/interface numbering)\\n   - LoopBack0 becomes lo0.0\\n3. The \"is-level level-1\" from Huawei is translated to \"level 1 disable\" in Juniper, which means disabling level-2 (making it level-1 only)\\n4. The IS-IS instance number (1 in Huawei) isn\\'t explicitly needed in Juniper\\'s basic configuration.\\n\\nNote: The network-entity and other IS-IS parameters would need additional configuration in Juniper to fully match the Huawei setup.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis level 1 disable'}]]": 1,
