    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##  \\n\\nExplanation:  \\n- In Juniper, IS-IS is enabled per interface under the `protocols isis` hierarchy.  \\n- The equivalent of `isis enable 1` is specifying the interface (with Juniper\\'s naming convention, e.g., `ge-1/0/0.0` for GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and `lo0.0` for LoopBack0).  \\n- Juniper doesn\\'t require an explicit IS-IS instance number (like \"1\") in basic configurations, as it defaults to a single instance.  \\n\\nNote: If the IS-IS instance must be explicitly set (uncommon in basic deployments), you would add:  \\n`set protocols isis instance-name 1`  \\nbut this isn\\'t typically required for direct equivalence.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##  \\n\\nExplanation:  \\n- In Juniper, IS-IS is enabled per interface under the `protocols isis` hierarchy.  \\n- The equivalent of `isis enable 1` is specifying the interface (with Juniper\\'s naming convention, e.g., `ge-1/0/0.0` for GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and `lo0.0` for LoopBack0).  \\n- Juniper doesn\\'t require an explicit IS-IS instance number (like \"1\") in basic configurations, as it defaults to a single instance.  \\n\\nNote: If the IS-IS instance must be explicitly set (uncommon in basic deployments), you would add:  \\n`set protocols isis instance-name 1`  \\nbut this isn\\'t typically required for direct equivalence.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.